description:
Summary: Several enterprise customers reported simultaneous request storms that manifested as long tail timeouts and a spike of 5xxs across Hosted and some Dedicated bridge paths. Investigation shows a converging set of conditions: upstream proxy header trimming caused idempotency token loss for a subset of SDK clients; this led to amplified client retries, which saturated per-host connection pools and triggered delayed KV-cache compaction on the runtime nodes. The compaction and heavy tail latencies then caused more in-flight requests to time out and retry, producing a cascading 5xx wave.

Root cause analysis: (1) Edge proxy (frontdoor) occasionally strips a custom idempotency header when requests are re-proxied after a TLS termination edge; (2) SDKs without robust jitter/entropy fallback repeatedly re-issued at fixed intervals, amplifying load; (3) Runtime's KV eviction policy used an LRU heuristic that performed poorly under sudden access pattern shifts, causing long stalls while compacting large KV pages; (4) Dedicated-routing path sometimes failed to honor fallthrough to lightweight model variant, keeping heavy slowpath active under surge.

Proposed fix: three coordinated changes across stack: 
- Implement a graded retry suppressor at the API gateway that tracks per-tenant short-window retry rates and returns a 429 with Retry-After or a synthetic lightweight degrade token when threshold exceeded;
- Patch SDK guidance and server-side idempotency tolerant path: add a fallback to compute a synthetic idempotency seed from request body hash when header missing, documented in SDK advisory;
- Adjust runtime KV compaction to incremental background compaction with capped per-cycle CPU and an early yield to avoid long tail stalls;
- Add an explicit graceful-degradation fallback to light/quantized model variant when node latency percentiles cross SLO thresholds; add transparent transparent routing to dedicated pools to shift burst traffic to Dedicated reserve if available.

Rollout plan: stage changes in this order to reduce blast radius: 
1) Deploy gateway suppressor as a feature-flagged service-level policy in edge-canary region (10% tenants) with monitoring hooks;
2) Release SDK advisory and server-side synthetic idempotency seed in the same canary so clients lacking header are spared amplification;
3) Deploy runtime KV incremental-compaction to canary nodes;
4) Enable model-variant fallback with canary and automated rollback on regression.

Acceptance criteria: see acceptance_criteria field.

Notes: cross-team coordination needed with SDK team, Edge/Frontdoor, Serving runtime, and Dedicated routing. Immediate mitigation (next 24h) is to apply tenant-level temporary admission caps for the most impacted customers and an emergency support page with recommended client-side backoff parameters.
